        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_017H2Axdc18noiFuuSNsaqSj",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022pages/concepts/centralized-configuration-control.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Centralized Configuration Control\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path decisions express a shared principle of controlling configuration at a single composition point.\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Centralized-Configuration-Control\\n  - tech/dotnet\\n  - security\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: Both credential-scoping and runtime-paths pages explicitly describe their respective centralizations; the shared principle across both is a natural inference from their parallel structure and codependency.\\nreview_date: 2025-07-30\\n---\\n\\n## The Principle\\n\\nCredential scoping and runtime-path resolution both enforce the same architectural principle: **configuration decisions must originate in a single, controlled place rather than being rediscovered or re-decided throughout the codebase.**\\n\\n## How They Connect\\n\\n**[[runtime-paths]]** centralizes all file-system location resolution in a single startup path resolver. Every location the Hub needs \u2014 wiki root, data directories, the secrets file, agent instruction directories \u2014 is determined once at startup from configuration sources (command line, environment, config file, or defaults) and validated there.\\n\\n**[[credential-scoping]]** uses that centralization to protect sensitive credentials. The secrets file location is known to the path resolver; the secrets loader reads from that one known location; credentials are injected only into child-process environments at spawn time, never held in the Hub\u0027s own process.\\n\\n## Why They Depend on Each Other\\n\\n- **Credential scoping needs path centralization**: If file paths were scattered across the codebase, the secrets file location could be accessed from multiple places, or the Hub might discover and read the secrets file into its own process environment (breaking the isolation). A centralized resolver ensures a single, controlled access point.\\n\\n- **Path centralization becomes security-critical for credentials**: Runtime-path resolution is generally a maintainability practice (single source of truth), but it becomes a security requirement when the resolved location contains sensitive data. The credential case demonstrates why this pattern matters beyond convenience.\\n\\n## Generalization\\n\\nRuntime-path resolution is broader than just credentials \u2014 it governs all runtime locations. But the security requirement for credential scoping makes clear why centralizing configuration decisions is fundamental, not optional: distributed configuration discovery creates both maintainability debt and security risk.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Now I can see the connection. Let me draft the synthesis and then preserve it:\n\nBoth [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] describe the same architectural principle: **centralizing and controlling where sensitive or critical configuration originates, rather than letting it scatter across the codebase.**\n\nHere\u0027s how they relate:\n\n- **Credential Scoping** isolates the Anthropic API key by ensuring it is injected only into child-process environments at spawn time \u2014 never residing in or inherited from the Hub\u0027s own process environment. This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking the credential.\n\n- **Runtime Path Resolution** ensures that every file-system location the Hub touches at runtime \u2014 including the secrets file itself \u2014 is resolved in a single composition point at startup. No other part of the codebase reads locations directly or rediscovers them independently.\n\nThe connection is this: **credential-scoping depends on runtime-paths.** The secrets file location cannot be scattered throughout the codebase; it must be known and resolved in the single path resolver. That resolver then feeds a known location to the secrets loader, which reads the credentials and injects them only into child processes. If paths were decentralized, the credential-scoping model would break \u2014 different code might try to read secrets from different locations, or the Hub might accidentally leak the credential by holding it in its own environment while discovering paths.\n\nConversely, **runtime-paths is more general than just credentials**. It governs all locations (wiki root, data directories, databases, agent instruction directories) \u2014 but the credential case is where path centralization becomes security-critical rather than merely a maintainability win.\n\nI\u0027ll preserve this connection as a new Synthesis Page:",
