      "assistant_text": "The Synthesis Page itself has been successfully created. Here\u0027s what I found:\n\n## The Connection\n\n**Credential scoping and runtime-path decisions express the same underlying principle: centralizing configuration control at a single composition point.**\n\nFrom [[credential-scoping]]: The Anthropic API key is never held in the Hub\u0027s own process environment. Instead, a secrets loader reads it from a \u0060.env\u0060 file at startup and injects it only into spawned child processes (Ingest and Query agents). This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking the credential.\n\nFrom [[runtime-paths]]: Every file-system location the Hub needs at runtime\u2014including the secrets file itself\u2014is resolved in exactly one place at startup. The path resolver reads configuration, validates it, and provides a single set of resolved paths to the entire application. No other code rediscovers locations independently.\n\n## How They Depend on Each Other\n\n**Credential scoping needs path centralization**: If paths were scattered across the codebase, different parts might try to read the secrets file from different locations, or the Hub itself might load credentials into its own environment\u2014breaking the security boundary. The centralized resolver ensures a single, controlled access point.\n\n**Path centralization becomes security-critical for credentials**: Runtime-path resolution is generally a maintainability practice, but it becomes a hard security requirement when the resolved locations contain sensitive credentials. The credential case shows why this pattern is fundamental.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/centralized-configuration-control]]. (The index and log catalog updates encountered formatting issues, but the Synthesis Page itself has been created and is accessible.)",
